ntent(inout) :: me integer(kind=int64), intent(in) :: i logical, intent(in) :: negative","tags":"","url":"module/text_buffer_module.html"},{"title":"astro_partials_module – halo","text":"Analytic partial derivatives for trajectory sensitivities (state transition\n  matrices): gravity gradients, and the time derivatives of the two-body rotating\n  frame transformation. These are general astrodynamics routines (they do not depend on the rest of HALO),\n  intended to be moved into the Fortran Astrodynamics Toolkit eventually: pines_gravity : spherical harmonic gravity acceleration and its gradient\n    (the 3x3 matrix of second partials of the potential), with Pines' nonsingular\n    formulation. It uses the same unnormalized coefficient arrays as the toolkit's geopotential_model_pines ( cnm , snm ), so it can be used with that class. pointmass_gravity_gradient , third_body_gravity_gradient . two_body_rotating_frame_matrices : the 6x6 inertial-to-rotating state\n    transformation of the toolkit's two_body_rotating_frame (and its inverse),\n    and their exact time derivatives. Pines' formulation With and the direction cosines , the potential is where , are the derived\n  Legendre functions, and . Every term is a\n  polynomial in , so there are no singularities (e.g., at the poles). Treating as a function of and of (as\n  independent variables), with , and the projection (since ): References S. Pines, \"Uniform Representation of the Gravitational Potential and its\n    Derivatives\", AIAA Journal, Vol. 11, No. 11, 1973, pp. 1508-1511. J. L. Spencer, \"Pines' Nonsingular Gravitational Potential Derivation,\n    Description and Implementation\", NASA CR-147478, 1976. Uses halo_kinds_module module~~astro_partials_module~~UsesGraph module~astro_partials_module astro_partials_module module~halo_kinds_module halo_kinds_module module~astro_partials_module->module~halo_kinds_module iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~halo_kinds_module->iso_fortran_env Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~astro_partials_module~~UsedByGraph module~astro_partials_module astro_partials_module module~halo_module halo_module module~halo_module->module~astro_partials_module module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule halo_eclipse_opt_submodule module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->module~halo_module Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: zero = 0.0_wp real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: one = 1.0_wp real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: two = 2.0_wp real(kind=wp), private, parameter :: three = 3.0_wp Functions public pure function pointmass_gravity_gradient (r, mu) result(grad) Gravity gradient of a point mass at the origin: . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: r position vector [km] real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: mu gravitational parameter [km&#94;3/s&#94;2] Return Value real(kind=wp), dimension(3,3) public pure function third_body_gravity_gradient (r, rb, mu) result(grad) Gradient of the third-body acceleration , , with respect to the spacecraft position (the indirect term does not depend on it): .\nThis is also the gradient of Battin's equivalent form (the same function). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: r spacecraft position vector [km] real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: rb third-body position vector [km] real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: mu third-body gravitational parameter [km&#94;3/s&#94;2] Return Value real(kind=wp), dimension(3,3) private pure function point_gradient (x, mu) result(grad) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: x real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: mu Return Value real(kind=wp), dimension(3,3) private pure function outer (a, b) result(c) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: a real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: b Return Value real(kind=wp), dimension(3,3) private pure function cross (a, b) result(c) Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: a real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: b Return Value real(kind=wp), dimension(3) private pure function cross_matrix (w) result(x) [w x]: x*v = w cross v Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: w Return Value real(kind=wp), dimension(3,3) Subroutines public pure subroutine pines_gravity (r, nmax, mmax, re, mu, cnm, snm, acc, grad) Spherical harmonic gravity acceleration, and optionally its gradient , with Pines' formulation\n(see the module documentation). Includes the central (point mass) term. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: r position vector [km] integer, intent(in) :: nmax degree to use (>=0) integer, intent(in) :: mmax order to use (0 to nmax ) real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: re body reference radius [km] real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: mu body gravitational parameter [km&#94;3/s&#94;2] real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:,0:) :: cnm unnormalized C coefficients cnm(n,m) real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:,0:) :: snm unnormalized S coefficients snm(n,m) real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(3) :: acc acceleration [km/s&#94;2] real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional, dimension(3,3) :: grad gravity gradient [1/s&#94;2] public pure subroutine two_body_rotating_frame_matrices (rv, rdot, vdot, m, mdot, n, ndot) The 6x6 state transformations of a two-body rotating frame (as defined by the\ntoolkit's two_body_rotating_frame ), and their exact time derivatives. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(6) :: rv state of the secondary w.r.t. the primary [km, km/s] real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: rdot time derivative of rv(1:3) [km/s] real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(3) :: vdot time derivative of rv(4:6) [km/s&#94;2] real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional, dimension(6,6) :: m inertial to rotating real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional, dimension(6,6) :: mdot dM/dt [1/s] real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional, dimension(6,6) :: n rotating to inertial real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional, dimension(6,6) :: ndot dN/dt [1/s]","tags":"","url":"module/astro_partials_module.html"},{"title":"halo_eclipse_opt_submodule – halo","text":"Eclipse optimization: moves a converged solution out of the Earth's shadow. Starting from the converged solution x0 , it finds the closest continuous trajectory\n(in halo's scaled variables) that stays out of the shadow near the eclipses: minimize subject to the continuity constraints (halo's) and, for each segment that comes near the shadow, with the SQP solver sqpopt . is the\ncubic shadow model (Williams et al., 2023), which is smooth inside and outside the\nshadow. are its values at samples every eclipse_opt_sample_step seconds\nfrom the segment's start, across the part of the segment near the shadow, and KS is\ntheir Kreisselmeier-Steinhauser aggregate , : a smooth lower bound of the smallest sample (within of it), so the constraint keeps every sample at or above . One row per segment, not one per sample: the samples of a segment all\ndepend on the same few variables (its start node), and a row for each would make the\nQP's working sets dependent (sqpopt's direct method then cycles). Following the 2023 paper's process, the constraints are added only where the trajectory\ncomes near the shadow, and the trajectory is checked again after each optimization (with\nnew rows where needed). Each pass ends with a Newton solve of the continuity constraints\nand the eclipse rows at their bound, as equalities ( eclipse_opt_newton_finish ), to\nbring the defects back to the usual level without undoing the eclipse fix. The continuity rows of the Jacobian are halo's analytic ones. The eclipse rows (7\nnonzeros each: the epoch and state of the segment's start node; the samples are at fixed\noffsets from the start) are computed by central differences, propagating only that\nsegment. See docs/eclipse_optimization_plan.md . Uses sqpopt_problem_module iso_fortran_env sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_qp_solver_module sqpopt_options_module sqpopt_module Ancestors: halo_module module~~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule~~UsesGraph module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule halo_eclipse_opt_submodule iso_fortran_env iso_fortran_env module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->iso_fortran_env module~halo_module halo_module module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->module~halo_module sqpopt_module sqpopt_module module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->sqpopt_module sqpopt_options_module sqpopt_options_module module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->sqpopt_options_module sqpopt_problem_module sqpopt_problem_module module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->sqpopt_problem_module sqpopt_qp_solver_module sqpopt_qp_solver_module module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->sqpopt_qp_solver_module sqpopt_types_module sqpopt_types_module module~halo_eclipse_opt_submodule->sqpopt_types_module bspline_module bspline_module module~halo_module->bspline_module ddeabm_module ddeabm_module module~halo_module->ddeabm_module fortran_astrodynamics_toolkit fortran_astrodynamics_toolkit module~halo_module->fortran_astrodynamics_toolkit json_module json_module module~halo_module->json_module module~as