riod. A trial point of the\noptimization can be far off. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( eclipse_opt_data ), intent(in) :: d real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x Return Value logical pure function ks_aggregate (ks, rho) result(agg) The KS aggregate of values ks : .\nOf the segments' KS rows, it is exactly the KS of all their samples. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: ks real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: rho Return Value real(kind=wp) function row_ks (mission, seg, row, t0, tf, x0_rotating, ok) result(ks) The KS aggregate of the shadow function at a row's samples, for the given start epoch,\nfinal epoch [days] and initial rotating-frame state (a copy of the segment is propagated,\nwith output every eclipse_opt_sample_step seconds). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( mission_type ), intent(in) :: mission type( segment ), intent(in) :: seg type( eclipse_row ), intent(in) :: row real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: t0 real(kind=wp), intent(in) :: tf real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(6) :: x0_rotating logical, intent(out) :: ok false if the segment has none of the row's samples (e.g., a trial point\nthat moved its epochs far): the row can't be evaluated there Return Value real(kind=wp) Subroutines subroutine eclipse_fc (x, f, c, status, data) The objective and the constraints for sqpopt: halo's continuity constraints, then the\neclipse rows. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x real(kind=wp), intent(out) :: f real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: c integer, intent(inout) :: status class(*), intent(inout), optional :: data subroutine eclipse_gjac (x, g, jac_val, accuracy, status, data) The gradient of the objective and the Jacobian values for sqpopt: halo's analytic\nJacobian, then the eclipse rows (central differences). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name real(kind=wp), intent(in), dimension(:) :: x real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: g real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: jac_val integer, intent(in) :: accuracy integer, intent(inout) :: status class(*), intent(inout), optional :: data subroutine eval_rows (d, ks, jac, ok, only) The eclipse rows (and, optionally, their partials, in the order of the rows' columns),\nfor the x already in the segments. The rows (segments) are done in parallel. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( eclipse_opt_data ), intent(inout) :: d real(kind=wp), intent(out), dimension(:) :: ks real(kind=wp), intent(out), optional, dimension(:) :: jac logical, intent(out), optional :: ok false if a row couldn't be evaluated (see row_ks ) logical, intent(in), optional, dimension(:) :: only evaluate only these rows (the others' ks are 0,\nand their partials are not set) subroutine newton_finish (d, x) The Newton finish ( eclipse_opt_newton_finish ): from the optimization's point, solve\nhalo's continuity constraints and, as equalities , the\neclipse rows that are at or near their bound ( plus eclipse_opt_newton_active_tol ), with halo's minimum-norm Newton solver (nlesolver, with\nhalo's sparse linear solver). The optimization's point is near feasible but its\ncontinuity isn't converged; a solve of the continuity alone ( eclipse_opt_polish ) moves\nthe trajectory back into the shadow, but with the active rows held at their bound it\ncan't. A row left free that falls below its bound is added to the equalities, and the\nsolve repeated (from where it ended). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( eclipse_opt_data ), intent(inout), target :: d real(kind=wp), intent(inout), dimension(:) :: x the optimization's point (halo's scaled variables); replaced by the solution subroutine newton_func (me, x, f) The Newton finish's functions: halo's continuity constraints, then KS - phi_min of\nthe active eclipse rows. A point that can't be evaluated (outside the splined\nephemeris, or an integrator failure) gets large values,